`,s.jsx(B,{items:[{q:"Give the YOLO output tensor shape and explain each term for v1.",a:"S x S x (B*5 + C). For v1, S=7 grid cells per side, B=2 boxes per cell each contributing x, y, w, h and a confidence — that is 5 numbers — and C=20 class probabilities shared across the cell. So 7 x 7 x 30 = 1,470 numbers, encoding 98 candidate boxes, all from one forward pass."},{q:"Which cell is responsible for an object, and which of its B predictors?",a:"The cell containing the object's centre, regardless of how far the box extends beyond it. Within that cell, the predictor whose current box has the highest IoU with the ground truth takes responsibility, which over training makes the B predictors specialise in different shapes."},{q:"Why is x,y normalised to the cell but w,h normalised to the whole image?",a:"Because x,y are an offset inside the responsible cell, so they naturally lie in [0,1) and can be squashed by a sigmoid — that keeps the prediction translation-equivariant across cells. Width and height, though, can easily exceed one cell, so they are expressed as a fraction of the image; using the cell as the unit would put the target far outside a convenient range."},{q:"Why does the v1 loss regress the square roots of width and height?",a:"To make the penalty relative rather than absolute. Plain squared error charges the same for a 5-unit width error on a 25-unit box and on a 200-unit box, but the first ruins the IoU and the second is invisible. Under the square root those cost 0.21 and 0.031 — about a 7x difference — so small boxes get proportionally larger gradients. Later versions get the same effect more cleanly with a log parameterisation."},{q:"Two objects fall inside the same grid cell in YOLO v1. What happens and why?",a:"One of them is lost. The cell holds only one set of class probabilities and can commit to a single object, so the second is simply not representable — an architectural ceiling, not a training shortfall. It is the reason v1 struggles with flocks and crowds, and why v2 attached predictions to anchors with their own per-box class scores."}]}),`
